NAME
       ticket-request - ask a Windows domain for service tickets (Cyber Range)

SYNOPSIS
       ticket-request -d DOMAIN -u USER [-p PASSWORD] DOMAIN-CONTROLLER

DESCRIPTION
       In a Windows domain any signed-in account may ask the domain controller
       (TCP port 88) for a ticket to a service, such as a database that runs
       under a service account. Part of the ticket is protected with that
       service account's password. ticket-request lists the domain's service
       accounts and prints a ticket for each in the range's own format:

           $rangetgs$svc_sql$5f0c...

       hashcrack(1) can try a word list against those tickets offline, without
       touching the domain again. That only works when the service account has
       a weak password: long, random service passwords (or managed service
       accounts, which change theirs by themselves) defeat it.

       This is a simplified, pretend version of the real protocol: it only
       works on the range's pretend domain.

OPTIONS
       -d DOMAIN     the domain (its DNS name or short name)
       -u USER       any domain account you know the password of
       -p PASSWORD   its password (ticket-request asks if it is left out)

EXAMPLES
       ticket-request -d corp.example -u jlee -p sunshine 10.10.20.10 > tickets.txt
       hashcrack tickets.txt

SEE ALSO
       hashcrack(1), winrm-shell(1), smbclient(1)
